    "excerpt": "America added record solar capacity again this year, and batteries are turning sunshine into an around-the-clock resource. But the grid's biggest bottleneck isn't generation anymore \u2014 it's the wires.",
    "featured": false,
    "body": "\n<p>From the air, the transformation is unmistakable. Solar farms now checker the deserts of the Southwest, float on reservoirs in California and sprawl across farmland from Texas to Ohio. In 2026, solar is no longer the alternative energy story \u2014 it is the energy story.</p>\n<h2>Record-Breaking Growth</h2>\n<p>Utility-scale solar accounted for the largest share of new US electricity-generating capacity for the fourth consecutive year. Panel prices, driven down by global manufacturing scale, have made solar the cheapest electricity in history in most American markets. Corporate procurement is accelerating the trend: Fortune 500 companies racing to meet clean-energy commitments now sign power-purchase agreements that anchor entire projects.</p>\n<h2>The Battery Revolution</h2>\n<p>The industry's most important innovation isn't a panel \u2014 it's a battery. Grid-scale storage installations have multiplied, allowing solar power generated at noon to be delivered at 8 p.m., exactly when demand peaks. In several states, battery fleets now respond to grid emergencies faster than natural-gas plants, a role unthinkable five years ago.</p>\n<h2>The Transmission Bottleneck</h2>\n<p>Yet the grid's Achilles' heel persists: transmission. Thousands of gigawatts of clean energy projects sit in interconnection queues, waiting years for access to congested power lines. Permitting reform, new regional grid planning and upgraded high-voltage corridors have become the industry's top policy priorities. The paradox of 2026 is that America can generate clean power far faster than it can move it.</p>\n<h2>Jobs, Towns and Trade-Offs</h2>\n<p>Solar now employs more Americans than coal, oil and gas extraction combined in several states. Rural counties lease farmland for panels, generating steady income for farm families. At the same time, debates over land use, supply-chain dependence on imported components and the pace of fossil-plant retirements continue to shape statehouse politics.</p>\n<p>The direction, however, is no longer in dispute. The question that defined the last decade \u2014 <em>can</em> renewables power America? \u2014 has been replaced by a harder one: how fast the country can build the wires, batteries and markets to let them.</p>\n"
