    "excerpt": "Electric vehicles have crossed from early adoption to the mainstream. Prices are falling, ranges are rising and the charging map is filling in \u2014 but the transition's toughest miles are still ahead.",
    "featured": false,
    "body": "\n<p>The American driveway is going electric \u2014 not with a bang, but with a quiet hum. In 2026, electric vehicles account for a steadily rising share of new-car sales, and the industry's center of gravity has shifted from proving the technology to perfecting the ownership experience.</p>\n<h2>The Price Parity Tipping Point</h2>\n<p>The single biggest barrier \u2014 cost \u2014 is crumbling. Battery prices have fallen dramatically, pushing more EV models below the average transaction price of a new gasoline car. Leasing has accelerated adoption further, and a robust used-EV market now offers sub-$20,000 entry points. Analysts expect sticker-price parity across most mainstream segments within two to three years.</p>\n<h2>The Charging Map Fills In</h2>\n<p>Range anxiety is yielding to a subtler metric: charging reliability. Federal infrastructure funding and automaker partnerships have expanded fast-charging corridors across rural America, while standards harmonization means a single plug type now works nearly everywhere. Apartment dwellers and urban drivers remain the toughest use case \u2014 a problem cities are attacking with curbside chargers and parking-policy reform.</p>\n<h2>What Buyers Should Know</h2>\n<p>Veteran EV owners offer consistent advice: think in terms of total cost of ownership, not sticker price. Fuel savings, reduced maintenance and tax incentives routinely offset thousands of dollars over five years. Cold-weather performance and towing capacity have improved but still require honest math for truck buyers.</p>\n<h2>The Roadblocks</h2>\n<p>Challenges remain real: dealership readiness, grid capacity for fast charging, tariff-shaken supply chains and political whiplash on emissions rules. The industry's resilience test will come if incentives are withdrawn before the market fully matures.</p>\n<p>Still, the trajectory mirrors smartphones circa 2009 \u2014 the debate has shifted from whether the transition happens to how fast. America's garage, one quiet revolution at a time, is already changing.</p>\n"
