    "excerpt": "A decade after cord-cutting began, the streaming industry has rediscovered the bundle \u2014 and the American viewer is once again deciding what a fair price for television really is.",
    "featured": false,
    "body": "\n<p>The streaming revolution ate the television industry, and then, quietly, the television industry ate the streaming revolution. In 2026, the great unbundling has reversed into the great re-bundling \u2014 and the American living room is the negotiating table.</p>\n<h2>The Bundle Strikes Back</h2>\n<p>After years of subscriber churn and mounting content costs, media giants converged on an old solution wearing new clothes: the bundle. Joint ventures now package multiple streaming services \u2014 once bitter rivals \u2014 into discounted mega-bundles sold through platforms, carriers and retailers. The pitch is nostalgia with a login: everything you loved about cable, minus the cable box, the contract and the remote with forty buttons.</p>\n<h2>Ads Are the Business Model</h2>\n<p>The industry's pivotal bet \u2014 that viewers would tolerate advertising for lower prices \u2014 has been decisively validated. Ad-supported tiers now drive the majority of new sign-ups, and advertising revenue has become the sector's growth engine. The economics inverted almost overnight: subscription revenue bought scale; advertising revenue buys profit.</p>\n<h2>Sports Rights Rule Everything</h2>\n<p>Live sports remain the last universal appointment television, and the rights wars reflect it. Tech platforms now sit alongside broadcasters at every major auction, streaming everything from NFL Sunday afternoons to niche college conferences. For leagues, the golden era has no visible end; for fans, following a favorite team now requires a spreadsheet of subscriptions.</p>\n<h2>The Viewer Verdict</h2>\n<p>Consumers have become sophisticated arbitrageurs \u2014 rotating subscriptions monthly, sharing household plans and rediscovering, of all things, free ad-supported television. Total viewing time has never been higher, even as patience for price increases wears thin.</p>\n<p>The industry's endgame is becoming clear: television isn't dying \u2014 it's being repriced. The winners will be the companies that remember the product was never the pipe, the bundle or the algorithm. It was always the story.</p>\n"
