    "excerpt": "College athletes are now paid professionals in every meaningful sense. How America's most beloved amateur institution became a billion-dollar industry \u2014 and what it means for the future of the game.",
    "featured": false,
    "body": "\n<p>The Saturday ritual looks unchanged \u2014 the bands, the tailgates, the fourth-quarter roar. But beneath the pageantry, American college sports completed a metamorphosis: the athletes on the field are now, in every economic sense, professionals.</p>\n<h2>How We Got Here</h2>\n<p>The chain reaction began with name, image and likeness (NIL) rights in 2021, accelerated through court rulings that dismantled amateurism restrictions, and culminated in direct revenue-sharing settlements that route a share of athletic-department income \u2014 television money above all \u2014 directly to players. The quarterback throwing touchdowns on your alma mater's field may now earn more than the university's assistant professors.</p>\n<h2>The New Economics</h2>\n<p>The numbers are staggering. Major programs operate nine-figure athletic budgets; collectives negotiate endorsement portfolios for seventeen-year-old recruits; and agents have become as common on campus as strength coaches. Star players in football and basketball command seven-figure valuations, while Olympic-sport athletes build niche brands that outlast their eligibility.</p>\n<p>The consequences cut both ways. Athletes finally share in the wealth they generate \u2014 a correction decades overdue. But the arms race has strained athletic departments, widened the gap between power conferences and everyone else, and forced small colleges to reconsider which sports they can afford to field.</p>\n<h2>What Fans Are Watching</h2>\n<p>On the field, the product has arguably never been better: veteran-laden rosters, empowered quarterbacks and transfer-portal drama that makes the offseason a second season. Purists mourn the erosion of loyalty; realists note that loyalty was always an artifact of the old system's restrictions.</p>\n<h2>The Unfinished Business</h2>\n<p>Employment status, unionization, Title IX balance, and federal legislation remain unresolved. Congress continues to dangle \u2014 and defer \u2014 a national standard. Meanwhile, the fans keep filling the stands, the networks keep writing the checks, and the definition of a \"student-athlete\" continues its rewrite in real time.</p>\n<p>Whatever the label, one fact is settled: the game changed, permanently, and everyone involved \u2014 players, fans and universities \u2014 is still learning the new rules.</p>\n"
